insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at)
values
  ('working-full-time-and-caring-for-someone', 'Working Full Time and Caring for Someone', 'Appointments in your lunch break, phone calls in meetings, and no slack anywhere. How to make two full-time commitments fit, and what to tell work about it.', 'You take the call in the stairwell because your mother has fallen again. You have a meeting in nine minutes and a form to fill in that closes today.

Nobody at work knows the half of it, and the people at home think you have more flexibility than you do. Both jobs assume they are your only one.

Why this particular squeeze is so tiring

Care does not fit in the gaps between things. It arrives at nine fifteen on a Tuesday, in office hours, from people who only work office hours.

Doctors, pharmacies, social services, banks, the benefits office: all open exactly when you are supposed to be somewhere else. So the care comes out of your lunch breaks, your annual leave and your evenings, and the evenings were where your rest used to be.

And the mental load runs all day underneath the work. Even on a quiet week, part of your attention stays on your phone, which is why this is exhausting in a way that neither job alone would be.

Get the invisible work written down

Most people carrying this have never seen the whole of it in one place, and seeing it changes what you do next.

Spend twenty minutes listing everything: medication, appointments, transport, shopping, meals, laundry, money and paperwork, phone calls, the visits themselves, the being-on-call. Put a rough time next to each, weekly.

Two useful things come out of it. A total, which is usually far bigger than you expected and explains why you are tired. And a column of items that do not actually need you specifically, which is the start of the next step.

Hand over what does not need to be you

The tasks that only you can do are fewer than it feels like. The rest are candidates.

Repeat prescriptions can often be delivered. Shopping can be ordered. A cleaner for two hours is cheaper than the weekend you lose doing it. Pharmacies will blister-pack medication. Day centers, lunch clubs and volunteer driving services exist in most places and are badly underused.

Paid help feels extravagant until you compare it to the alternative, which is you doing it at eleven at night. And where money is the obstacle, a benefits check is worth an hour of somebody''s time: carer''s allowance, attendance allowance and local authority support are frequently unclaimed.

Ask for a needs assessment from your local social services and a carer''s assessment for yourself. Both exist, both are free to ask for, and plenty of people carry this for years without knowing that the second one is about them.

Tell work something, even if you do not tell them everything

The instinct is to say nothing, in case it counts against you. That usually costs more than it protects.

A manager who does not know thinks you are unreliable. A manager who knows can often offer things you did not realize were available: a later start, a fixed day at home, a block of unpaid leave, flexible hours, or simply not being first in line for the early meeting.

You do not owe anyone the details. I am one of the people looking after a parent, and there are some appointments I cannot move is enough.

And find out what you are entitled to, which is more than most people assume: unpaid time off for emergencies involving a dependant, flexible working requests, and, in many workplaces, a carer''s policy nobody has ever read. Human resources will know even if your manager does not.

Protect the boundary in both directions

The hard part is that both jobs expand to fill whatever you give them.

So set a couple of plain rules and keep them: no care calls in the two hours when you need to concentrate, except emergencies. No work email after you get back from a visit. One evening a week that belongs to neither.

Tell the family what the rules are, rather than hoping they will guess. People generally cooperate with a specific request and ignore a general sense that you are stretched.

And share the load where there is anyone to share it with. Siblings who live further away can take the phone calls, the paperwork, the ordering and the research, which is real work and can be done from anywhere.

Keep one thing that is yours

This is the first casualty, and the one that matters most over a long stretch.

Pick something small and fixed: a swim, a class, a walk with a friend, one coffee on a Saturday morning. Put it in the calendar as an appointment rather than a nice idea, because an unscheduled plan is the one that gets eaten.

Carer groups are worth more than they sound, too. Not for advice, mostly, but because everybody there already understands the thing you are too tired to explain.

When to get help for yourself

Please talk to a doctor if you are not sleeping, if you are getting ill repeatedly, if you are drinking more, or if you have stopped being able to feel much about any of it. Those are the standard signs of being past capacity rather than evidence that you are failing at this.

A therapist is worth considering if resentment, guilt or dread have become the loudest part of your day, or if you are grieving someone who is still here. And if the person you care for is becoming unsafe, or you are reaching the end of what you can do, say so plainly to their doctor or to social services rather than waiting until something breaks. Asking for more help is part of the job, not an admission about you.', array['Caregiving', 'Work']::text[], 0, '2026-10-12T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('grazing-all-evening-without-noticing', 'Grazing All Evening Without Noticing', 'Dinner finished at seven and you have been eating ever since. What is usually driving the evening grazing, and the small changes that work better than willpower.', 'You were not hungry at nine, or at half past ten, and you ate anyway. Crackers, then the end of the cheese, then whatever was in the cupboard behind the pasta.

By the time you go up you feel slightly ill and quite annoyed with yourself, and you have no clear memory of deciding to do any of it.

This is one of the most common patterns there is, and almost none of it is about self-control.

What is usually driving it

Four things, and most people have two or three.

The day was too long. Evening grazing is often the first unsupervised, undemanded hour you have had, and food is the fastest available comfort.

You did not eat enough earlier. People who skip breakfast, work through lunch or eat a small dinner are not making a moral choice at ten at night; they are hungry, and the body is very good at collecting what it is owed.

You are tired. Short sleep reliably increases appetite and reduces the part of you that would otherwise say no.

And it is a habit in the literal sense: a cue, an action and a reward, repeated so often that it runs without a decision. Sitting on the sofa is the cue. The eating is automatic.

Eat more during the day, not less

This is the counterintuitive bit, and it is usually the single biggest change.

If the evening is when all the eating happens, the daytime is where to look. A proper breakfast with some protein in it, a lunch that is actually a lunch, and a dinner big enough to be a meal rather than a gesture.

People who try to balance a grazing evening by eating less in the day almost always make it worse, because they arrive at seven in the evening genuinely depleted and then spend four hours catching up.

So do the opposite for a week and see what happens. Most people find the evening quietens down on its own.

Change the environment rather than your resolve

Willpower at ten at night, after a long day, is the weakest tool you have. Make the environment do the work instead.

Keep the grazing foods out of sight or out of the house. Not because they are bad, but because open packets on a counter get eaten by anybody. Put them in a cupboard, in a tin, on a high shelf.

Buy the small packet. Put a portion in a bowl rather than eating from the bag, which is the one change most people notice immediately, because the bag has no end in it.

And have one easy, decent thing available for when you genuinely are hungry: fruit, yogurt, nuts, toast, leftovers. Hungry at ten is not a crime, and something that counts as food beats four handfuls of whatever.

Break the automatic part

Habits run on cues, so put a small pause where the cue is.

Clear the table and wash up, which signals that eating is finished. Make a hot drink, because that is often what the hand is reaching for. Brush your teeth earlier than usual, which is a surprisingly effective full stop.

Then put something in the gap. The grazing usually fills an hour that has nothing else in it, and an hour with no plan reliably becomes an hour of eating. A bath, a jigsaw, something with your hands, a phone call, a short walk round the block.

If you snack in front of the television, try eating only at the table for a week, and see how much of it turns out to be the sofa rather than the food.

Ask what the evening is for

Sometimes the honest answer is that food is the only pleasant thing in the day, and that is worth taking seriously rather than treating as greed.

If the day is all duty, the evening will look for a reward, and food is cheap, immediate and always there. The lasting fix is not stricter rules; it is having something else to look forward to.

So put one small genuinely enjoyable thing in the evening on purpose. It sounds too simple to work and it changes the pattern more than another resolution does.

Drop the running commentary

The cycle that does the real damage is not the eating; it is the evening of eating followed by a morning of self-attack, which usually produces a restricted day, which produces a bigger evening.

So when it happens, note it plainly and stop there: I grazed all evening, I was tired and I had a tiny lunch. That is information you can act on. I have no self-control is not.

One evening is an evening. There is nothing to make up for the next day.

When to take it to someone

Please talk to a doctor or a therapist if the eating feels out of your control rather than simply automatic, if you eat a great deal in one go and feel distressed afterward, if you are doing anything to compensate, or if you are eating in secret.

Those patterns are common, they are not a character problem, and they respond well to proper help; a doctor can also check whether anything physical is driving the appetite, and can point you toward someone who works with this.

And if food has become the main way you manage feelings you cannot otherwise get through, a counselor is a reasonable place to take that. It is a far more ordinary reason to ask for help than most people assume.', array['Food', 'Habits']::text[], 0, '2026-10-12T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-your-body-changes-what-you-can-do', 'When Your Body Changes What You Can Do', 'An injury, an illness or just getting older, and suddenly the stairs are a decision. How to grieve what your body used to do while getting on with the life you have.', 'You used to run for the bus. Now you plan around whether there is somewhere to sit, and you have started looking up how far the walk is from the station.

Whether it arrived overnight with an injury or crept in over five years, this is one of the larger changes a person goes through, and it is treated as a practical problem when most of it is not.

It is a loss, and it is allowed to be one

People around you will talk about adaptations and aids and being positive. Underneath that, something has gone, and it deserves to be named.

Not only the activity itself but what came with it: being the capable one, the person who carries things, the one who says yes to the hill walk. Spontaneity goes too, replaced by planning, which is its own quiet grief.

And it is not a single loss you get over. It turns up again at each new thing you discover you cannot do, which is why people are surprised to feel wretched about it years later.

Letting it be sad is not giving up. People who refuse to acknowledge the loss tend to get stuck fighting their own body, which is exhausting and does not win.

Find out what is actually true now

Fear fills in the gaps, so get the real information rather than the version you have assumed.

Ask the specific questions: what is likely to change, what will not, what makes it worse, what is safe, what helps. A physical therapist is often more useful here than anyone, because their job is exactly what you can do and how to do more of it.

And ask how other people with the same thing manage, including the practical tricks. There is usually a body of ordinary knowledge that nobody tells you and that a patient group or a charity can hand over in an afternoon.

Separate what is caused by the condition from what is caused by stopping

This matters more than almost anything else here.

When something hurts or is hard, people reasonably do less of it, and then lose strength and stamina on top of the original problem. A good deal of what feels like decline is deconditioning, and that part is reversible.

So find the floor and work up from it slowly, with help if you can get it. Ten minutes, three times a week, of something gentle, done consistently, does more than a heroic effort once a month, and it is the opposite of the push-until-it-hurts-then-rest-for-a-week cycle most people fall into.

Pacing is the skill, and it is genuinely a skill: doing a bit less than you could on a good day so there is still something left tomorrow.

Use the aids early

People treat a stick, a shower rail, a scooter or a blue parking permit as a defeat and put them off for years.

What those things actually do is buy back range. A stick is the difference between a walk and no walk. A rail is the difference between showering standing up and dreading it. A scooter on the long bits means you still get to the market with everyone else.

The question is not whether you need it. It is what you get to do if you have it.

Rebuild the life around what remains

The temptation is to keep doing the old things badly and then stop doing them. The better move is to work out what you were getting from a thing and find another route to it.

If it was being outdoors, that might become a flatter route, a bench halfway, a boat, a garden. If it was company and effort, perhaps swimming, seated classes or coaching the team you used to play for. If it was feeling capable, something with your hands, or the kind of competence that has nothing to do with legs.

Keep the parts that still work busy. Identity rebuilds around use rather than around what you used to be.

Handle the other people

You will get unwanted advice, false cheer, and the relative who insists you would be fine if you tried harder.

Have one short line ready, and do not argue: this is how it is now, and I have a good team looking after it. Repeat it and change the subject.

Tell the people close to you what actually helps and what does not. Most of them want to get it right and are guessing. Asking before helping, not moving your things, carrying the bag without a comment, slowing down without announcing it: those are specific and easy for someone to do.

And say yes to the modified version of things more often than feels dignified. Being at the party sitting down is better than not being at the party.

When to get help with the mood, not just the body

Please talk to a doctor or a therapist if the low mood has been there for weeks, if you have stopped doing the things you still could do, if pain and sleep are feeding each other, or if you are drinking more to get through the evenings.

Low mood alongside a change in health is extremely common, treatable, and not something to wait out; mentioning it is also how you find the services that exist for exactly this, from pain programs to rehabilitation and peer groups. A counselor who works with health changes is a good place to put the grief, and the grief is the part people most often try to carry alone.', array['Life Transitions', 'Resilience']::text[], 0, '2026-10-12T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('checking-your-body-for-signs-something-is-wrong', 'Checking Your Body for Signs Something Is Wrong', 'Pressing the same spot, taking your pulse, looking it up again. Why checking makes the worry stronger rather than settling it, and how to put it down without ignoring real symptoms.', 'You found it three weeks ago and you have checked it every day since, sometimes twice an hour. You have pressed it, photographed it, compared it with the other side and read nine pages about it.

You know that checking does not help, which is the strange part. And the next time the thought arrives, your hand goes there anyway.

Why checking cannot give you what you want

The aim of a check is certainty, and a check cannot produce certainty, because bodies are lumpy, asymmetrical and changeable.

So each check gives you a few seconds of relief and then a new question. Was it bigger yesterday? Is that a different color? Did it hurt before I pressed it, or because I did?

Worse, checking creates the very evidence it is looking for. Press anything repeatedly and it gets tender. Take your pulse while worried and it is fast. Keep swallowing to test your throat and your throat starts to feel tight. Search a symptom and you will find the frightening explanation long before the boring one.

And the relief is what keeps the habit alive. Your brain learns that checking fixes the fear, briefly, so the urge comes back sooner and stronger.

Notice the whole family of checking

It is rarely only looking. The behaviors that do the same job include taking your pulse, weighing yourself repeatedly, examining the same spot, swallowing to test, breathing deeply to see if it hurts, reading medical pages, and asking someone whether it looks all right.

Reassurance from other people is a check too, which is why asking your partner for the tenth time does not help either of you.

So is the opposite: avoiding looking at all, avoiding exercise in case of a symptom, or putting off an appointment in case of what you might hear. Both ends run on the same fear.

Get it properly checked once

This is the part that makes everything else possible.

Make the appointment, tell the doctor everything you have noticed, and ask directly: do you need to see this again, and what would you want me to come back for?

That last question is the one worth getting an answer to. A plan you can hold on to — come back if it changes shape, grows, bleeds, or if it is still there in six weeks — gives you something to follow other than your own monitoring.

Then take the answer as the answer. Going back repeatedly for the same thing is another kind of checking, and more scans rarely settle a fear that reassurance cannot settle.

Put the checking on a diet

Stopping dead usually fails. Reducing it on a schedule usually works.

Decide a specific allowance: once a day, at a set time, for thirty seconds. Then cut it to every other day, then twice a week, then stop. Write it down, because without a plan you will negotiate with yourself at the moment you are least able to.

In between, when the urge arrives, let it be there without acting. It will rise, peak and fade, usually in a few minutes, and each time you let it pass without checking, the urge turns up a little weaker.

Keep your hands busy, go into another room, put the phone in a drawer. The aim is to get through the wave, not to feel calm.

And stop looking things up. Delete the history, block the sites if you need to. Searching is the most reliable way to turn a slightly odd sensation into an afternoon of terror.

Say something accurate instead

Not reassurance, which your mind will argue with, but something true.

I have had this checked, and my job now is to let the plan do its work. Or: this is my alarm, not new information. Or simply: not today, I check on Fridays.

Flat and factual beats positive. You are not trying to convince yourself nothing is wrong; you are declining to relitigate it every hour.

What this is not

None of this means ignoring your body, and it does not mean a symptom is imaginary.

Real things need looking at: a lump that is new or growing, bleeding that is not normal for you, a mole that is changing, losing weight without trying to, a cough that will not clear, chest pain on exertion, anything your doctor told you to report. Have them seen.

The difference is between seeing something once, properly, and monitoring it continuously. The first is sensible. The second gives you no new information and costs you your week.

When to get help with the worry itself

Please talk to a doctor or a therapist if checking is taking up a large part of your day, if you cannot accept reassurance for more than an hour, if you have had the same thing looked at several times, or if the fear is keeping you from work, sleep or the people around you.

This is one of the best-understood patterns in therapy and it responds well, usually in a small number of sessions, by doing roughly what is above with someone keeping you honest about the checking. Say to the doctor or counselor you see that the problem is the checking and the fear rather than the symptom; that sentence will get you to the right kind of help quickly.', array['CBT', 'Body']::text[], 0, '2026-10-12T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('choosing-between-two-things-you-want', 'Choosing Between Two Things You Want', 'Not a choice between bad options, but between two good ones you cannot have both of. Why that is harder than it sounds, and how to choose without spending a year on it.', 'The job in the city or the house near your sister. The course or the savings. The second child or the work you have just got going again.

Both are good. That is the problem. A choice between something you want and something awful is not really a choice; a choice between two things you want means something real gets left behind either way.

Why the good version is harder

With a bad option, the decision makes itself. With two good ones, every reason for one is a reason against something you also wanted.

So people do what feels responsible: gather more information, make a list, ask around. And none of it resolves anything, because there is no fact out there that makes the loss disappear. This is not a question with a right answer waiting to be found.

Underneath it sits the quiet hope of a third option where you lose nothing. Waiting for that one is how people spend a year not deciding.

Name the loss out loud

Strangely, the fastest way through is to stop trying to make the decision painless.

Write the sentence plainly for both sides. If I take the job, I will miss my niece growing up. If I stay, I will probably never do this kind of work again.

People avoid writing that down because it stings, and it is exactly what clarifies things. Quite often one loss turns out to be clearly heavier than the other, and the choice makes itself the moment both are on the page rather than circling in your head.

Work out which one you are really choosing between

A surprising number of these decisions are not actually about the two options on the table.

Ask what each one gets you. Security, or being near people. Being known, or being free. Ambition, or a quieter life. Then ask which of those you have been short of lately, because that is usually the better guide than a list of pros and cons.

And check whether you are choosing between the thing and the idea of the thing. The job comes with a commute and a manager; the house comes with a mortgage and your sister''s opinions. Imagine a boring Wednesday in each version rather than the brochure.

Ask the two useful questions

The first: which one would I regret not trying?

Regret is lopsided. People rarely regret the attempt that did not work; they regret the thing they never found out about. That asymmetry is worth real weight, particularly when one option has a closing window and the other does not.

The second: which can I come back to?

Many pairs look symmetrical and are not. You can often take the job and move later; you cannot always go back to the course, the house, or the year when your parents were well. Where one door closes and the other stays open, go through the closing one.

Set the deadline and let it be enough

Give it a date, not a condition. Deciding on the fifteenth is a plan; deciding when I feel sure is a way of waiting forever, because certainty is not what this kind of choice produces.

Tell one person the date, which makes it real, and say out loud what you will decide with: what you know by then, and what you have written down.

If the two are genuinely level by the deadline, that is useful information rather than a failure. When the scales will not tip, the honest move is to pick, usually the one with the closing door, and put your effort into making it work instead of into weighing it.

Then stop running the other version

The decision is only half of it. What spoils a good choice afterward is keeping the other one open in your head.

So stop pricing it, stop checking the listings, stop asking how the person who took the other path is doing. Every comparison makes the life you chose feel provisional, and a provisional life is hard to commit to.

And expect a wobble. A few weeks in, the loss will show up properly and you will wonder whether you got it wrong. That is grief about the road not taken, not evidence of a mistake, and it passes if you let it be what it is.

Make the thing you chose good. That does more for how this turns out than the choice itself did.

When it is bigger than this decision

If you have been stuck for months, if the stuckness is costing you sleep or straining a relationship, or if every decision in your life goes like this, it is worth looking at with someone rather than trying harder alone.

Please talk to a therapist or a counselor if the fear of choosing wrongly is strong enough to keep you in a life you do not want, or if the decision has become a loop you cannot step out of. And if the weight of it has flattened your mood for weeks rather than days, mention that to a doctor too; it is a common and treatable thing to come in with, and easier to decide anything once it lifts.', array['Decision Making', 'Goals']::text[], 0, '2026-10-12T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
on conflict (slug) do update set
  title        = excluded.title,
